[2026-11-16T09:14:13.623Z] [requirements-st-b1522d] [request] Observation: {"ok":true,"sections":[{"label":"--- xcoder-ai-dojo/dojo-blueprint.md (lines 1-200 of 386) ---","content":"# Xcoder AI Dojo — Complete Blueprint\n\nBaseline: repository version **0.1.4**, reviewed **14 November 2026**.\n\nThis document defines the Dojo's product, implemented architecture, operating model, and proposed evolution. **Current** means present in this repository; **proposed** means future work, not a shipped capability. Source code takes precedence where older handoff documents differ. External agent internals and the Studio Docker supervisor are outside this repository.\n\n## 1. Product purpose\n\nXcoder AI Dojo is a Windows-first, PyQt6 desktop development environment for mixed-language workspaces. It brings source editing, project execution, developer utilities, AI assistance, coding-agent delegation, and voice interaction into one application.\n\nThe central workflow is: open a workspace, understand the code, edit or delegate a task, run the project, inspect results, and iterate. A workspace can be a single project or a parent folder containing multiple projects with different runtimes.\n\nThe intended users are individual developers working across Python, web, Java, shell, and SQLite projects; developers integrating APIs and local services; and users who want conversational or spoken assistance alongside their editor.\n\nThe Dojo is a desktop workbench. This repository does not implement a hosted collaboration service, cloud account system, billing platform, learning curriculum, or production deployment control plane.\n\n### Product principles\n\n- Keep the active workspace and command working directory explicit.\n- Preserve source encodings and line endings when editing existing files.\n- Keep slow or failure-prone work outside the Qt UI process where supported.\n- Distinguish ordinary chat, bounded workspace inspection, and agent execution.\n- Make running work, failures, cancellation, and missing dependencies visible.\n- Store application state locally and disclose when content leaves the machine.\n- Keep source and packaged worker behavior aligned.\n\n## 2. Scope and capability boundaries\n\n| Area | Current capability | Boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Workspace | Folder tree, nested project discovery, file tabs | No workspace collaboration service |\n| Editor | Highlighting, line numbers, find, save, encoding preservation | No full language-server or debugger integration established here |\n| Execution | File/project runners and shared console | Required language tools must be installed separately |\n| Markdown | Live preview, links, local images | Embedded HTML and remote images are skipped |\n| SQL | SQLite scripts, ephemeral or selected database | Not a general database administration suite |\n| Developer tools | Maven, npm, pip, terminals, Docker, network, SSH, HTTP | Uses local tools and their configured access |\n| AI task board | Durable text requests, queue, task controls | Does not execute workspace tools |\n| JARVIS | Chat, memory compaction, workspace inspection, delegation | Separate execution path from the task board |\n| Coding agents | Local SDLC, LiteBro, QA CLI integration | External packages own agent behavior |\n| Agents catalog | Pinned package information and Docker health check | Docker supervisor/job orchestration is not included |\n| Voice | Local recognition, local/online speech, device settings | Hardware and speech-backend availability vary |\n| Distribution | Windows portable package and per-user installer | Signing and clean-machine acceptance remain release work |\n\n## 3. User experience\n\n### Application layout\n\nThe main window combines a workspace tree on the left, tabbed source editors in the center, a right-side tool rail and selected tool panel, a shared run console, and status information. Markdown can appear beside its source. The hub and JARVIS interface add themed artwork, assistant controls, transcript, microphone state, and audio feedback.\n\nAppearance setti
[2026-11-16T09:14:15.549Z] [requirements-st-b1522d] [stop=end_turn] [response] {"thought":"Good signal. Let me read the rest of the blueprint plus structural outlines of the core modules to pin down concrete features for the port.","action":{"tool":"read_multiple_files","input":{"files":[{"path":"xcoder-ai-dojo/dojo-blueprint.md","startLine":200,"endLine":386},{"path":"xcoder-ai-dojo/xcoder_ai_dojo/agent_tools.py","startLine":1,"endLine":90},{"path":"xcoder-ai-dojo/xcoder_ai_dojo/tool_commands.py","startLine":1,"endLine":80},{"path":"xcoder-ai-dojo/xcoder_ai_dojo/sdlc_tool.py","startLine":1,"endLine":70}],"maxTotalChars":18000}}}
[2026-11-16T09:14:15.573Z] [requirements-st-b1522d] [request] Observation: {"ok":true,"sections":[{"label":"--- xcoder-ai-dojo/dojo-blueprint.md (lines 200-386 of 386) ---","content":"\nJARVIS maintains interactive conversation memory separately from the durable task board. It can summarize accumulated messages, retain a bounded summary, and resume a request after successful compaction. Failed compaction preserves the previous chat. Do not treat this in-memory conversation as equivalent to durable task history.\n\nSupported inspection tools are `list_dir`, `glob`, `grep`, `read_file`, `read_multiple_files`, `read_file_lines`, `read_outline`, `validate_file`, `get_python_imports`, `git_status`, `git_diff`, and `git_log`.\n\nThe inspection boundary rejects absolute paths, traversal, disallowed path components and resolved escapes from the workspace. It excludes hidden names, selected generated directories and common credential/key filenames. Reads and searches are bounded: output is generally limited to 12,000 characters, multi-file reads to four files, line requests to 200 lines, and scans to 1,500 encountered files. These controls reduce exposure; they are not comprehensive secret detection.\n\nCurrent tool dispatch accepts one tool call per response. An inspection result is passed to a follow-up model request as data, with instructions to ignore instructions inside that data. This is a bounded request/observation/answer flow, not an unrestricted recursive tool loop.\n\n### Coding-agent delegation\n\nJARVIS can dispatch SDLC, LiteBro and QA tasks through `SdlcRun`. Direct coding/fix controls use the same integration. File-specific fixes validate that the target is inside the active workspace. Dispatch rejects a changed workspace context.\n\nThe adapter discovers Node and external agent entry points, supports its native/shell/WSL execution paths, supplies the workspace and task, streams output, and handles stopping. Agent concurrency is requested as one. LiteBro receives its secure-mode argument. These CLI flags do not establish a desktop-wide permission or isolation system.\n\nPer-run provider settings are written to temporary configuration, and conflicting inherited provider variables are removed. This temporary configuration contains the API key in plaintext while needed by the external CLI; permanent Windows key protection does not apply to that temporary representation.\n\nThe Docker-oriented Agents catalog is separate: it lists pinned SDLC, Jarvis, QA, LiteBro, Origin, Litecoder and DNA packages and can check the engine. It does not implement the absent Studio supervisor.\n\n## 8. Voice and HUD\n\nRecognition uses Qt Multimedia capture and Vosk locally. The English recognition model is prepared by the launcher/build process and bundled in Windows releases. Legacy/manual model placement is described in the voice integration handoff.\n\nAfter an end-of-speech pause, recognized text is appended to any draft and submitted, unless speech review is enabled. Microphone listening pauses during assistant work, review, and speech playback, and resumes only when the relevant UI state allows it. This helps prevent the assistant's voice from becoming a new request.\n\nMicrophone and speaker default to off; saved choices apply on later launches. Settings expose device selection, nine voice presets, online/local mode, pitch, speed, and volume. Voice preview uses unsaved values; saving persists the selected configuration.\n\nOnline synthesis uses `edge-tts` and sends reply text to Microsoft. Local synthesis depends on the platform's available speech backend. Recognition is local, but recognized text sent as an assistant prompt follows the configured LLM's network path.\n\nHUD animation and visual themes must reflect actual listening, working, and playback states. They are presentation components, not independent AI runtimes.\n\n## 9. Persistence and migration\n\nCanonical Dojo data root: `~/.xcoder/dojo`.\n\n| Location | Contents and handling |\n| --- | --- |\n| `settings.ini` | QSettings appearance and other s
