[2026-11-16T09:18:11.179Z] [requirements-st-b1522d] [stop=end_turn] [response] {"thought":"Document verified in place at .xcoder/backlog/requirements-st-b1522d.md with 21 acceptance criteria, 10 non-goals, 12 technical constraints, 13 security constraints, all 7 sections, no leftover sentinels, and the probe file removed. Emitting the final answer.","action":null,"final_answer":{"summary":"Explored the reference product (xcoder-ai-dojo: a PyQt6 Python desktop IDE with editor, workspace tree, run console, bounded workspace-inspection agent, agent-CLI delegation, voice and Docker catalogue) and wrote a requirements document for a TypeScript port into a new xcoder-dojo/ folder. The document turns the vague input into a concrete checkable outcome: a two-command-startable app that completes the loop open folder -> tree -> edit with highlighting and line numbers -> save preserving BOM/CRLF -> run and read console output -> agent inspects workspace with allowlisted tools -> agent delegates a coding task with streamed output. It states 10 explicit non-goals (voice, Docker catalogue, installer/signing, Butler persona, LSP/debugger/Git UI/SSH, multi-user, pixel-parity art, durable task board, data migration, non-English voice), 12 technical constraints (TypeScript strict, Electron main/preload/renderer isolation, CodeMirror 6 or Monaco, separate data root ~/.xcoder/dojo-ts, OS-protected key store, encoding fidelity, reference read bounds, bounded one-call-per-turn agent loop), 13 binding security constraints mapped to the secure-architect bylaws (injection delimiting, allowlisted tools only, schema-validated output, sandbox enforced in code, human approval for writes/agent runs, sandboxed execution, redacted audit log, cost controls, supply chain), 4 assumptions, 21 testable acceptance criteria, 5 open questions with defaults, and a reference-module traceability table.","deliverable":{"goal":"Build xcoder-dojo: a self-contained TypeScript implementation of the Xcoder AI Dojo 