[2026-11-16T09:18:26.095Z] [design-st-2c7624] [request] Observation: {"ok":true,"sections":[{"label":"--- xcoder-ai-dojo/dojo-blueprint.md (lines 1-90 of 386) ---","content":"# Xcoder AI Dojo — Complete Blueprint\n\nBaseline: repository version **0.1.4**, reviewed **14 November 2026**.\n\nThis document defines the Dojo's product, implemented architecture, operating model, and proposed evolution. **Current** means present in this repository; **proposed** means future work, not a shipped capability. Source code takes precedence where older handoff documents differ. External agent internals and the Studio Docker supervisor are outside this repository.\n\n## 1. Product purpose\n\nXcoder AI Dojo is a Windows-first, PyQt6 desktop development environment for mixed-language workspaces. It brings source editing, project execution, developer utilities, AI assistance, coding-agent delegation, and voice interaction into one application.\n\nThe central workflow is: open a workspace, understand the code, edit or delegate a task, run the project, inspect results, and iterate. A workspace can be a single project or a parent folder containing multiple projects with different runtimes.\n\nThe intended users are individual developers working across Python, web, Java, shell, and SQLite projects; developers integrating APIs and local services; and users who want conversational or spoken assistance alongside their editor.\n\nThe Dojo is a desktop workbench. This repository does not implement a hosted collaboration service, cloud account system, billing platform, learning curriculum, or production deployment control plane.\n\n### Product principles\n\n- Keep the active workspace and command working directory explicit.\n- Preserve source encodings and line endings when editing existing files.\n- Keep slow or failure-prone work outside the Qt UI process where supported.\n- Distinguish ordinary chat, bounded workspace inspection, and agent execution.\n- Make running work, failures, cancellation, and missing dependencies visible.\n- Store application state locally and disclose when content leaves the machine.\n- Keep source and packaged worker behavior aligned.\n\n## 2. Scope and capability boundaries\n\n| Area | Current capability | Boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Workspace | Folder tree, nested project discovery, file tabs | No workspace collaboration service |\n| Editor | Highlighting, line numbers, find, save, encoding preservation | No full language-server or debugger integration established here |\n| Execution | File/project runners and shared console | Required language tools must be installed separately |\n| Markdown | Live preview, links, local images | Embedded HTML and remote images are skipped |\n| SQL | SQLite scripts, ephemeral or selected database | Not a general database administration suite |\n| Developer tools | Maven, npm, pip, terminals, Docker, network, SSH, HTTP | Uses local tools and their configured access |\n| AI task board | Durable text requests, queue, task controls | Does not execute workspace tools |\n| JARVIS | Chat, memory compaction, workspace inspection, delegation | Separate execution path from the task board |\n| Coding agents | Local SDLC, LiteBro, QA CLI integration | External packages own agent behavior |\n| Agents catalog | Pinned package information and Docker health check | Docker supervisor/job orchestration is not included |\n| Voice | Local recognition, local/online speech, device settings | Hardware and speech-backend availability vary |\n| Distribution | Windows portable package and per-user installer | Signing and clean-machine acceptance remain release work |\n\n## 3. User experience\n\n### Application layout\n\nThe main window combines a workspace tree on the left, tabbed source editors in the center, a right-side tool rail and selected tool panel, a shared run console, and status information. Markdown can appear beside its source. The hub and JARVIS interface add themed artwork, assistant controls, transcript, microphone state, and audio feedback.\n\nAppearance settin
